1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:04,908
Who is Vishnu? Ask, and you may be given ten answers.

2
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:13,250
He is the preserver of the world, the keeper of its balance, often painted blue: the colour of the sky and the sea.

3
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:21,423
And whenever that balance fails, he comes down into the world, each time in a new form. These are his avatars.

4
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:29,541
First, a fish. In a great flood, Matsya towed the boat of Manu, the first man, to safety.

5
00:00:33,950 --> 00:00:39,226
A tortoise. Kurma held a mountain on his back, while the ocean was churned.

6
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:50,453
A boar. Varaha dived to the bottom of the sea, and lifted the drowned earth on his tusks.

7
00:00:54,950 --> 00:01:06,140
A man-lion. A demon king could not be killed by man or beast, by day or by night, inside or out. So Narasimha came at dusk, on a doorstep.

8
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:16,263
A dwarf. Vamana asked for three steps of land, and covered the universe in two.

9
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:23,979
A warrior with an axe: Parashurama.

10
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:34,180
A prince with a bow: Rama, hero of the Ramayana.

11
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:44,621
A cowherd with a flute: Krishna, who taught the Bhagavad Gita.

12
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:55,885
A teacher of peace. In many lists, the Buddha. In some others, Balarama.

13
00:02:01,450 --> 00:02:06,729
And one still to come: Kalki, on a white horse, at the end of this age.

14
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:20,944
Some readers see a pattern in them: from the water, to the shore, to the land; from animal, to human, to sage.

15
00:02:21,650 --> 00:02:31,056
So who is Vishnu? Perhaps the answer is in the word itself. Avatar means a descent. He is the one who keeps coming down to help.

16
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:34,677
Again, and again.
